      "output": {
        "message": "Here are three practical ways to handle this: 1. **Compare against your own baseline.** Measure your progress against where you were a month or two ago, rather than where someone else says they are right now. Your starting point, coursework load, and pace are different from theirs.\n2. **Account for the highlight reel.** People usually talk about their high scores, completed projects, or quick study sessions—not the hours they struggled or things they didn't understand. You're comparing your full, messy process to their edited summary.\n3. **Redirect the comparison into one immediate action.** When you catch yourself measuring against someone else, use that as a cue to do one concrete thing for your own work: read five pages, outline one problem, or organize your notes for tomorrow. Start by noticing the specific moments it happens most often—like right after class or during group chats—so you can pause before spiraling into it.",
